<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tonga</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kingdom of Tonga</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tonga is an island country in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polynesia">Polynesia</a>, in the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pacific-ocean">Pacific Ocean</a>, made up of 171 islands. It was first settled roughly 2,500 years ago by people of the Lapita culture, and later became the centre of the Tuʻi Tonga Empire, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thalassocracy">thalassocracy</a> that held parts of the Pacific from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fiji">Fiji</a> to Samoa and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niue">Niue</a>. Europeans reached the islands in 1616. From 1900 to 1970 Tonga was a British protected state under a Treaty of Friendship, but it never relinquished its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>, and in 1970 it joined the Commonwealth. In 2010 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/absolute-monarchy">absolute monarchy</a> gave way to a semi-constitutional one.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Lewanika led raids against the Ila and <b>Tonga</b> to the east in the 1880s, bringing back cattle and captives, but Ndebele raids from the south and enemies at his own court led him by the late 1880s to seek British protection.</blockquote><a href="/empire/barotseland">Barotseland · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Tonga</b> regained full independence on 4 June 1970, the 108th anniversary of George Tupou I’s 1862 code freeing commoners from forced service to their chiefs.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-tonga">Kingdom of Tonga · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Māori moved from rural communities to the cities on a large scale after the Second World War, and from the 1960s migrants from Samoa, the Cook Islands, <b>Tonga</b> and Niue made Auckland the largest Polynesian city in the world.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-zealand">New Zealand · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Force was used against protest: a largely non-violent protest by the Gwembe <b>Tonga</b> against hut tax in 1909–10 had been severely suppressed.</blockquote><a href="/empire/northern-rhodesia">Northern Rhodesia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In July 1777 James Cook watched the ʻinasi first-fruits ceremony at Muʻa and wrote that its occasion was the Tuʻi <b>Tonga</b> Paulaho and his son eating together, a thing never done before.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tu-i-tonga-empire">Tuʻi Tonga Empire · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>According to Albert Schütz, Marquesans colonised the archipelago in roughly 300 CE, followed by later waves from the Society Islands and Samoa-<b>Tonga</b>, and their languages in time became Hawaiian.</blockquote><a href="/language/hawaiian">Hawaiian · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
